      "filename": "Ford's Theatre (President Lincoln assassination site) (8446138851).jpg",
      "title": "File:Ford's Theatre (President Lincoln assassination site) (8446138851).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Ford%27s_Theatre_%28President_Lincoln_assassination_site%29_%288446138851%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ford%27s_Theatre_(President_Lincoln_assassination_site)_(8446138851).jpg",
      "width": 542,
      "height": 800,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "InSapphoWeTrust from Los Angeles, California, USA",
      "description": "In 1996, I made my first-ever visit to Washington, DC, using the Election Day long weekend, while I was a college student in New York City. I spent four days in the capital, and my emphasis was primarily to cover various topics that I had learned in my high school history and government/civics classes.\n\nAnd this is definitely a historical site: on the evening of 14 April 1865, here at Ford's Theatre, President Abraham Lincoln was watching a play in a private box, when a pro-Confederate sympathizer, John Wilkes Booth, walked in and shot Lincoln in the head. Lincoln died the next morning at a house across the street, just behind me. Lincoln had pushed for reconciliation as the main plank of the Reconstruction after the defeat of the Confederacy, but with Lincoln dead, Northern hardliners could impose harsher conditions on Southern states' re-admittance to the US to retaliate for the war losses, and the North-South tension, over racial and other issues, continues to this day.",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 4 November 1996, 12:45",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "fords-theatre-wk",
      "sourceGeohash4": "dqcj"
    }
